; The Lorentz factor's gamma - 1, written the way the algebra falls out:
;
;   gamma - 1 = (1 - sqrt(1-v*v)) / sqrt(1-v*v)
;
; For small v both operands of the subtraction approach 1, so it cancels and the
; relative error is unbounded in practice.  The fix is the conjugate, and unlike
; examples/guarded.fpcore this one needs no guard: the divisor it invents,
; 1 + sqrt(1-v*v), is provably in [1,2] on the whole box, so the rule fires
; globally.  Kept as the contrast case -- a large condition number does not on
; its own mean a conditional is needed.
;
; It is tempting to want the conjugate only where v is small, since that is
; where the seed cancels.  Measured over 3000 points per band, worst/mean ulps:
;
;   v in [1e-6, 0.01]   seed 8.2e11 / 2.2e10     conjugate 3.0 / 1.477
;   v in [0.01, 0.5]    seed   12219 / 540       conjugate 3.0 / 1.423
;   v in [0.5, 0.9]     seed     7.0 / 2.174     conjugate 4.0 / 1.477
;
; The conjugate wins in every band, so there is nothing to switch back to.  The
; general rule: the conjugate of a - c divides by a + c, so it has its own bad
; region at a = -c, and a conditional is needed only when the box holds both.
; Here a + c is in [1,2] throughout.  Compare lightcone-energy.fpcore, where
; a + p reaches zero at one end of the box and a - p at the other.
;
; v is held off zero because gamma - 1 vanishes there, and no rewrite gives a
; relative bound at a zero of the value.
(FPCore (v)
  :name "gamma - 1"
  :pre (<= 0.000001 v 0.9)
  (/ (- 1 (sqrt (- 1 (* v v)))) (sqrt (- 1 (* v v)))))
